Committed. Here is the report.

## What landed

--provider and --model resolve independently in parse_model_config, so a foreign id went to the wrong endpoint with no reconciliation. A new pure helper, model_provider_mismatch_warning(model, provider, base_url_given) -> Option<String> (src/cli.rs), is emitted once at resolution time, beside the existing unknown_model_warning.

## Visibility had to be widened — yes, by exactly one word

infer_provider_from_model was private (src/prompt_retry.rs:791), called from exactly one place (:681, retry/fallback) — the issue's claims verified at HEAD. I widened it to pub(crate) plus a doc comment saying why. Its body, its tests, and its one existing call site are untouched. That is the only change to prompt_retry.rs (+5 lines, gate-warned not failed).

The reason is not convenience: restating the family keyword table in the parser performs no read, so it has no edge to grep and would drift silently. cli.rs asks the same table.

## The "never guess" clause needed a *second* authority, not a re-read

infer_provider_from_model returns "anthropic" both for a real Claude id and for an id carrying no family keyword at all — its own return value cannot tell those apart, so it cannot implement "inference returns nothing". I used providers::model_is_known_for_provider (the known-model lists plus anthropic_preset) as the independent positive-attribution check, which makes the two agree by observation rather than by my typing hand.

## The four table rows as measured (end-to-end via the built binary, not just the unit test)

```
claude-fable-5-1     deepseek       base_url=false -> WARN: Model 'claude-fable-5-1' is an id from the anthropic family, but provider 'deepseek' is set, so requests go to https://api.deepseek.com/v1. Pass --provider anthropic if that is what you meant, or --base-url if the endpoint is deliberate (a gateway serving these ids).
deepseek-v4-flash    deepseek       base_url=false -> None
totally-unknown-model deepseek      base_url=false -> None
claude-fable-5-1     deepseek       base_url=true  -> None
```

Real-binary counts for my warning's text: 1, 0, 0, 0. The --base-url row is silent, and an aggregator/local-runtime extra row (openrouter/ollama/github/custom) is silent too — serving foreign ids is legitimate there. The silent rows are pinned with full-string assert_eq!, not contains, and every provider's own default model is asserted silent so --provider X with no --model is byte-identical.

## Two corrections to my own process, both worth recording

1. **My first positive control was vacuous. I added `// NEUTERED POSITIVE CONTROL` to an *existing* `return None;` — the mutation never landed, and the suite stayed green** while I was one step from reporting it as a passing control. That is the "did the mutation land?" failure my own notes name. Redone as an inserted early `return, it reddened by name (expected warning=true, got None`); a second control (inverting the positive-attribution clause) reddened the near-miss direction; both restored, one atomic mutate→run→restore each.

2. **The article bug was invisible in the unit test** — "a anthropic id" renders identically in a contains assertion. It only showed up in the pasted rows.

## Register, as the gate requires

• `src/cli.rs` 7397 → 7584 — past the 100-line fatal band, so fatal until pasted. The bulk is the emission-point table test.
• `src/prompt_retry.rs` 2713 → 2718 — inside the grace band, pasted too so the entry is the high-water mark.

## The `.github/workflows/*` discovery, named rather than "fixed"

All five workflows pass MODEL: ${{ secrets.MODEL || 'claude-opus-4-6' }} with no provider, while .yoyo.toml sets provider = "deepseek". So the harness does run the disagreeing pair (an anthropic id under a deepseek provider) — and by design it stays silent, because the API key fallback means no 401 actually occurs there. The warning fires only if secrets.MODEL is ever unset, which is the intended discovery. Untouched, as instructed.

All four checks pass: cargo fmt -- --check, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo build, cargo test (17 targets, 0 failures). Docs updated: docs/src/troubleshooting/common-issues.md gains the mismatch as a fourth cause of API errors, and ARCHITECTURE.md gains the per-file entry.
